# PREREG.md carries a published SHA-256 in PREREG.sha256, so its BYTES are the artifact,
# not just its text. Without this line git stores LF and checks out CRLF on Windows, which
# gives the same file two different hashes: a Linux or macOS reviewer computes one value and
# a Windows checkout computes another. Only one of them can match whatever is published, so
# the integrity record fails for most reviewers and reads as "they edited the frozen
# document", which is precisely the accusation a pre-registration exists to pre-empt.
#
# Pinning eol=lf makes `sha256sum PREREG.md` return the same value on every platform.
PREREG.md text eol=lf
PREREG.sha256 text eol=lf
PREREG-ERRATA.md text eol=lf
